 match so we'll just poke and  run away come back poke and run away  when the octopus come towards each other  to begin the fight they don't actually  face each other they approach sideways  the defense of octopus tries to face  away from the attacker until it's the  critical moment it knows there's no way  to avoid a fight really the one who's  waiting the last moment is defensive he  was the aggressor in a fight we'll  quickly and sharply flash bright black  on his arms when he is about to initiate  a fight ooh and already we're seeing  some action looks like this each other  right so now he's going to become is  approaching but not directly at it yeah  they're like almost completely  antiparallel and then right there yeah  like contacts and then their arms clash  together  so we've taken the first steps in  understanding fighting in the octopus  and you may be asking yourself why does  this even matter  well these types of curiosity based  research question can often lead to some  unexpected insights and discoveries and  we've learned a lot about ourselves from  studying marine animals squid have  taught us about how our neurons  communicate and the horseshoe crab has  taught us about how our eyes work so  it's not too far of a stretch to say  that some of these behaviors that we're  seeing in the California - spotted  octopus are similar to arms  [Music]
 

